MyGo v0.9.0: ship app extensions in Contents/PlugIns (macos.plugIns)

scripts/mygo.mjs fetches github.com/egoist/mygo@v0.9.0 from the Go module
proxy, applies this patch with `git apply` and builds cmd/mygo into the
user cache; the desktop packaging scripts run that CLI. Text before the
first `diff --git` line is ignored by `git apply`.

Why: the macOS desktop widget is a WidgetKit extension (.appex) that must
sit in Contents/PlugIns, signed with its own entitlements (App Sandbox and
the shared App Group) before the app is signed around it. MyGo 0.9 copies
resources into Contents/Resources only, and signs the app with
`codesign --force --deep`, which would sign any extension again with the
app's entitlements and drop its sandbox.

What it changes:
- config: `macos.plugIns: [{ path, entitlements? }]` (.appex, .plugin or
  .bundle; unique bundle names), typed in packages/cli/index.d.ts and
  documented in docs/configuration.md.
- writeBundle copies each plug-in into Contents/PlugIns (any build host,
  so cross-packaged bundles carry it too).
- signNestedCode signs Contents/Frameworks code and then each plug-in,
  with its entitlements file or `--preserve-metadata=entitlements`.
- codesign drops `--deep` for the app when plug-ins are configured;
  without plug-ins the arguments are unchanged.
- `mygo dev` re-signs when a plug-in's entitlements change and watches the
  plug-in bundles.
- tests: config validation, the copy into PlugIns, and (macOS only) the
  extension keeping its own entitlements after the app is signed.

Upstream status: not submitted. Drop this patch once a MyGo release has an
equivalent option.

